column12:
Idaho Power's Schedule 20, effective January 1, 2024, applies to customers with speculative high-density loads—such as cryptocurrency mining operations—that exhibit high energy use density and demand. These customers are subject to interruptible service during summer peak hours. The tariff aims to ensure that energy and infrastructure costs are appropriately recovered from high-risk, high-load customers.

Schedule 20 contains three rates and contract options. It is applicable to customers whose energy usage at one point of delivery exceeds 2,000 kWh per billing period, three or more times in 12 months. It is also applicable to and may be mandatory for customers who "have the ability to relocate quickly in response to short-term economic signals and meet four or more of..." seven criteria related to load and financial characteristics. Customers with >20,000 kW demand are ineligble and must make special contract arrangements.

Schedule 20 rates and contract options include:
Speculative High-Density Load Large Power Service Rates-- mandatory for customers registering metered demand >=1,000 kW three or more of the most recent 12 billing periods;
Speculative High-Density Load Large General Service Rates-- for customers that have not registered metered demand >=1,000 kW more than twice during the most recent 12 billing periods;
Applicable customers may optionally take service under a special contract for firm electric demand of 10 MW to 20 MW.
